   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "A past that no longer changes",
     "texto": [
      "To imagine a different past you need two forms with **haber**:\n\n- **Si** + **hubiera** + participle → the condition that did not happen.\n- **Habría** + participle → the result that did not happen.\n\nExample: **Si lo hubiera sabido, habría ido.** (I did not know, and I did not go.)\n\nIt is the «impossible» past: it can no longer be changed, only imagined."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "The three kinds of «si»",
     "texto": [
      "The whole system fits into three patterns:\n\n- **Possible**: si + present → present, future or command. **Si tengo** tiempo, **iré**.\n- **Unreal now**: si + imperfect subjunctive → conditional. **Si tuviera** tiempo, **iría**.\n- **Unreal in the past**: si + pluperfect subjunctive → conditional perfect. **Si hubiera tenido** tiempo, **habría ido**.\n\nSometimes the condition and the result speak of different times: **Si hubiera aceptado** el trabajo, **ahora viviría** en Chile.\n\nThe forms in **-ra** and in **-se** are equal: si hubiera sabido / si hubiese sabido. Never put the conditional after **si**: «si habría sabido» is a mistake.\n\nYou can also say **de** + compound infinitive: **De haberlo sabido**, habría ido."
     ],
